---
import ProjectLayout from '../../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
import Section from '../../../components/content/Section.astro';
import Figure from '../../../components/content/Figure.astro';
import Embed from '../../../components/content/Embed.astro';
import { imgdb as img } from '../../../data/imgdb';

const params = [
  { id: 'viscosity', label: 'Viscosity', step: 0.001, value: 0.5 },
  { id: 'pressure', label: 'Pressure', step: 0.001, value: 0.25 },
  { id: 'radius', label: 'Radius', step: 0.01, value: 0.5 },
  { id: 'velocity_dissipation', label: 'Velocity dissipation', step: 0.001, value: 0.25 },
  { id: 'density_dissipation', label: 'Density dissipation', step: 0.001, value: 0.25 },
];
---

<ProjectLayout id="stableshape">
  <div slot="cover" class="sim" data-sim>
    <canvas id="fluidsim_canvas" aria-label="Interactive fluid simulation. Click and drag to stir the fluid."></canvas>

    <p class="sim-caption label">Fig. 00 — Stable Fluids in WebGL2 · drag to stir</p>

    <details class="sim-params">
      <summary class="label">Parameters</summary>
      <form id="param_form" onsubmit="return false">
        {
          params.map((p) => (
            <label class="param">
              <span>{p.label}</span>
              <input type="range" id={p.id} min="0" max="1" step={p.step} value={p.value} />
            </label>
          ))
        }
        <fieldset id="display_radio">
          <legend>Display</legend>
          <label><input type="radio" name="display" value="dye" checked /> Dye</label>
          <label><input type="radio" name="display" value="velocity" /> Velocity</label>
          <label><input type="radio" name="display" value="pressure" /> Pressure</label>
        </fieldset>
      </form>
    </details>

    <p class="sim-fallback">This interactive demo needs WebGL2, which isn’t available in this browser.</p>
  </div>

  <Section title="In action">
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        StableShape is a Grasshopper plugin that turns fluid simulation into form. The live figure above runs the same
        Stable Fluids solver in your browser; below, the plugin at work inside Grasshopper.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Embed
      src="https://www.linkedin.com/embed/feed/update/urn:li:ugcPost:7340807801595641858?compact=1"
      title="StableShape demo video on LinkedIn"
      ratio="16 / 10"
    />
  </Section>

  <Section title="Algorithm framework">
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The core of StableShape is two classes: <code>StableFluid</code> and <code>ParticleSystem</code>. StableFluid
        manages the velocity and density fields, resolving the Navier–Stokes equations through diffusion, advection and
        pressure projection — the steps that keep the simulation stable.
      </p>
      <p>
        ParticleSystem operates on a mesh of particles connected by springs, handling tension and mesh
        reconstruction. By treating mesh vertices as particles carried by the stable velocity field, the framework
        achieves realistic fluid behavior.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure src={img('697160961404c8e205f11a2e')} alt="Diagram of the StableFluid and ParticleSystem classes" caption="Algorithm framework." plate />
  </Section>

  <Section title="From field to morphology">
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The project explores the transformation from scalar fields to morphology. In physics, a field is a quantity —
        scalar, vector or tensor — with a value at every point in space and time. Here the scalar field is fluid
        density.
      </p>
      <p>
        In three dimensions, the level sets of a scalar field are two-dimensional: iso-surfaces. These surfaces can
        represent a three-dimensional fluid in a way a 2D height field cannot — each one is a closed body, the
        three-dimensional equivalent of a contour line.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Morphogenesis methods">
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        StableShape offers two ways to generate form. The VDB method uses the Dendro plugin to convert density points
        into volumes; the mesh method uses the particle system to deform meshes. Both evolve complex fluid forms from
        simple primitives.
      </p>
      <p>
        Everything runs inside Grasshopper — grid construction, VDB processing, the StableFluid solver and the
        ParticleSystem solver — so designers can manipulate and watch forms evolve in real time.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure src={img('697161351404c8e205f11a62')} alt="Comparison of the VDB and mesh morphogenesis methods" caption="VDB and mesh methods." plate />
  </Section>

  <Section title="Implementation">
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The fluid solver runs in four stages: initialization, adding density, updating the velocity field and updating
        the density field. At each time step it resolves the Navier–Stokes equations through diffusion, projection and
        advection, keeping the simulation physically plausible and numerically stable.
      </p>
      <p>
        The mesh solver drives the particle system: mesh topology, particle movement, tension and reconstruction.
        Together they generate fluid forms that evolve over time, opening new possibilities for architectural design
        exploration.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure src={img('697160a71404c8e205f11a32')} alt="Grasshopper definition showing the implementation process" caption="Implementation process." plate />
  </Section>
</ProjectLayout>

<script>
  import '../../../scripts/stable-fluids.js';
</script>

<style>
  .sim {
    position: relative;
    height: min(88svh, 56.25vw);
    min-height: 24rem;
    background: #050505;
    color: #ecebe6;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  #fluidsim_canvas {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    cursor: crosshair;
    /* Vertical swipes still scroll the page on touch screens. */
    touch-action: pan-y;
  }

  .sim-caption {
    position: absolute;
    left: var(--gutter);
    bottom: var(--space-4);
    color: #b0afa8;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .sim-params {
    position: absolute;
    right: var(--gutter);
    bottom: var(--space-3);
    width: min(19rem, calc(100% - 2 * var(--gutter)));
    color: #ecebe6;
  }

  .sim-params summary {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: flex-end;
    gap: 0.5em;
    padding-block: var(--space-2);
    color: #ecebe6;
    cursor: pointer;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .sim-params summary::-webkit-details-marker {
    display: none;
  }

  .sim-params summary::after {
    content: '+';
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .sim-params[open] summary::after {
    content: '−';
  }

  #param_form {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
    padding: var(--space-4);
    border: 1px solid rgb(255 255 255 / 0.18);
    background: rgb(5 5 5 / 0.72);
    -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(8px);
    backdrop-filter: blur(8px);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
  }

  .param {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-1);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
  }

  .param input {
    width: 100%;
    accent-color: var(--accent);
  }

  fieldset {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    padding: var(--space-3) 0 0;
    border: 0;
    border-top: 1px solid rgb(255 255 255 / 0.18);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
  }

  legend {
    float: left;
    width: 100%;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-1);
    color: #8a8983;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-label);
  }

  fieldset label {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4em;
  }

  fieldset input {
    accent-color: var(--accent);
  }

  .sim-fallback {
    display: none;
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    place-content: center;
    padding: var(--gutter);
    text-align: center;
    color: #b0afa8;
  }

  .sim[data-unsupported] .sim-fallback {
    display: grid;
  }

  .sim[data-unsupported] .sim-params,
  .sim[data-unsupported] .sim-caption {
    display: none;
  }

  @media (max-width: 40rem) {
    .sim-caption {
      top: var(--space-4);
      bottom: auto;
    }
  }
</style>
